Wire standalone A-MSDU TX into the rtl8822cu USB xmit path (v5): N subframes.

Realtek only wired rtw_xmitframe_coalesce_amsdu() into the PCIe xmit path, and even
there it only ever aggregates a PAIR. On USB it has no caller at all, so the
`tx_amsdu` proc knob is inert.

This is the only aggregation available on this link. The peer is a userspace libusb
station that cannot emit a compressed BlockAck, so the AP's agg_enable_bitmap stays
0 permanently (80+ issue_addba_req, never established) and MAC efficiency sits near
40% of PHY. A-MSDU needs no BlockAck.

Changes:

1. rtw_xmitframe_coalesce_amsdu() now takes an ARRAY of queued frames instead of
   one, so N subframes ride in a single MPDU. The per-subframe write was already a
   self-contained block; it is now simply a loop. Pairs cap efficiency near 64%,
   whereas 6 subframes (the peer's advertised 7935-byte max A-MSDU) reaches ~84%.

2. New rtw_dequeue_xframe_amsdu_n() atomically dequeues 2..N aggregatable frames or
   nothing, under pxmitpriv->lock, stopping before the 7935-byte cap. All candidates
   come from one per-STA/per-AC servq, so the RA matches by construction and only
   the TID is compared.

3. It uses amsdu_ok_locksafe() rather than check_amsdu(). check_amsdu() calls
   rtw_get_stainfo(), which takes sta_hash_lock; holding that under pxmitpriv->lock
   is a lock-order inversion that throttled the whole xmit loop to ~12 iterations/s
   -- only the pre-pass's own aggregates got out, ~240 kbps of a 28 Mbps stream.
   amsdu_ok_locksafe() replicates every check_amsdu() test except the per-STA
   WLAN_STA_AMSDU_DISABLE lookup, which nothing in this driver sets.

Subframe count is runtime-tunable so N can be swept without rebuilding:
  echo 1 > /proc/net/rtl88x2cu/wlan0/tx_amsdu        (enable)
  echo 6 > /proc/net/rtl88x2cu/wlan0/tx_amsdu_rate   (max subframes; 0/1 => 2)
tx_amsdu_rate is only read by check_amsdu_tx_support() when tx_amsdu==2, so it is
free to repurpose. amsdu_debug_timeout counts builds with more than 2 subframes.

Regressions from v1-v4 that this must not reintroduce, all measured on real HW:
  - Hand-rolled single-frame sends: one leaked the xmit_buf (137x
    "xmit_status_check tx hang", STAs stopped associating), the other silently
    dropped ~92% of frames (tx_dropped=0, nothing in dmesg) -> black screen.
  - Returning _FALSE from the pre-pass: the caller does
    `if (ret == _FALSE) break;`, which breaks the entire TX drain loop
    (34 Mbps -> 0.3 Mbps, proven by toggling tx_amsdu alone).
  - Dequeuing speculatively and putting frames back: rtw_xmitframe_enqueue()
    appends at the TAIL, pushing EAPOL behind data so the station never completed
    the 4-way handshake.
  - Calling check_amsdu() under pxmitpriv->lock (see 3).

